WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.060
Chris Gore, Founder/Owner of Film Threat/Author/Filmmaker:
I hear these to me horror stories from friends

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:06.040
that have gotten themselves in north of a
hundred thousand dollars in debt from their

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.250
education.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:12.509
Film Courage: Is it a good idea to turn your
passion into a business?

00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:19.970
Chris: Oh I think I think if you are starting
a business that is not your passion that's

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:28.360
a mistake your your business must be something
you're passionate about is it distributing

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:36.070
documentaries is it a a new streaming service
is it a type of chocolate I mean whatever

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:42.500
it is if you I don't think a business can
succeed unless you're passionate about what

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:48.510
it is and I think it's important to have that
mission statement in your head I mean i'm

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:54.920
constantly altering it for film threat but
yeah I don't know how you could even be successful

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:58.810
in a business that you're not passionate about
but I also think it's important to be sober

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.670
minded

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:06.330
if you were passionate about crochet toilet
paper holders and there's not a market for

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:14.510
that it might fail i'm talking about hand
crocheted toilet paper holders that may be

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:22.450
the greatest business idea ever i'm going
to guess it isn't but I just you know whatever

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.290
it is that you're passionate about I think
I just don't see how you could succeed from

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:35.840
a business standpoint because there's so much
sacrifice involved in business it is you know

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:40.080
and for my friends that have very traditional
jobs I have friends that have nine to five

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.470
jobs they don't understand that i'm never
not working you know if it's 9 p.m on a Saturday

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:50.479
night and my website goes down I have to be
available to be able to solve that problem

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:56.720
right so and I have to be passionate enough
about it that it's not an interruption it's

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.720
something that i'm leaping to to take care
of it right but I do think it's also about

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:07.400
a balance there is freedom in having your
own business because I can decide well this

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:12.410
morning i'm you know it's 11 in the afternoon
i'm gonna go see a movie and I can do that

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:17.939
it's also part of my job sometimes but yeah
I really feel like if you don't have that

00:02:17.939 --> 00:02:23.120
passion then don't do that as as a job really
because once you do it it doesn't even feel

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:29.599
like work I mean my job doesn't I never even
describe it as work you know it's just it's

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:34.489
my purpose for being here when that teacher
said to you don't you want the nine to five

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:37.919
job i'm sorry that was high school that was
high school yeah okay and did they send a

00:02:37.919 --> 00:02:44.019
note home to say you know dear mrs gore by
the way your son no no I didn't we're worried

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:50.319
about him I didn't get pushback from that
but I do feel I feel betrayed by my high school

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:57.340
education from this standpoint I it was very
much focused on just making good workers for

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:03.299
certain types of jobs and I don't know if
traditional pathways for education are preparing

00:03:03.299 --> 00:03:10.551
preparing young people for for the types of
jobs that are out there I feel in a way it

00:03:10.551 --> 00:03:17.909
might it might be easier to just start your
own job I mean my pathway which is very non-traditional

00:03:17.909 --> 00:03:23.159
and i'm not recommending this i'm just telling
you as an example here's what I did I felt

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:28.670
somewhat betrayed by my high school education
in the sense that I took I was like got very

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:40.609
good grades in school a's and b's 3.5 3.6
plus gpa average it's I did well but I sort

00:03:40.609 --> 00:03:43.749
of stepped outside my comfort zone said I
want to be a writer I better learn to type

00:03:43.749 --> 00:03:49.139
so I took a typing class and I did not get
a good grade in typing I got a c and I got

00:03:49.139 --> 00:03:53.260
a c because I didn't type fast enough and
I thought the criteria was wait a sec I care

00:03:53.260 --> 00:04:00.499
about accuracy you know so I mean now I type
fairly fast but you know at the time I thought

00:04:00.499 --> 00:04:05.109
well why am I being knocked out like i'm actually
that's like outside of the required curriculum

00:04:05.109 --> 00:04:11.529
I also took a whole met class because I wanted
to cook right and I will say this just in

00:04:11.529 --> 00:04:18.630
the days where I was single and I cooked it
was as if I did a magic trick I do think that

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:23.420
developing skills that are useful in life
are really important and I meet too many of

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:29.190
my friends who don't have skills and I feel
like a lot of the skills that I developed

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:33.620
in high school and beyond were skills that
were either things that I chose to do and

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:38.280
maybe didn't succeed as well or they were
recreational for example when I was in college

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:42.060
I read a book on marketing called marketing
warfare that taught me almost everything I

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:48.200
needed to know about marketing brilliant it
it basically this book marketing warfare which

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:54.160
is an updated version taught me about the
rivalries between coke and pepsi mcdonald's

00:04:54.160 --> 00:05:02.000
and burger king the concept of free and how
that is used in marketing and that taught

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:08.330
me a lot also I read that book for fun I was
not assigned that book I just read it I also

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:13.380
read a book on advertising by george ogilvy
called ogilvy on advertising that was also

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:19.410
a recreational read I learned about fonts
I learned that white type against a black

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:24.600
background looks 10 larger among other sort
of useless pieces of information that are

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:29.110
kind of ingrained in my head about why things
work the differences between non-serif and

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:34.860
serif fonts graphic design so I tended to
expand my horizons by just recreationally

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:43.120
reading books that had nothing to do with
my chosen pathway which was filmmaking additionally

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:51.550
in terms of my education I grew up pretty
poor single mother raised us and I really

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:57.860
wanted to go to usc film school I really wanted
to go to usc why george lucas went there and

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:02.520
george lucas was my hero because he made star
wars and I want to go to the same school that

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:10.090
george lucas went well my family being poor
it wasn't even an option for me and I mean

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:14.810
I was disheartened I was going to this commuter
college wayne state university in detroit

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:22.280
and I was taking their film courses and recreationally
reading books about marketing and fonts and

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:27.530
it was I know it sounds strange but I really
want to glean all that knowledge and and when

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:33.460
I was I was just so disheartened and I remember
going on this kind of life-changing trip to

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:40.060
the west coast my mother took my sister and
I to go visit my uncle who lived in the San

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:43.950
Diego area and I begged her I said look I
know we're gonna go to like Disneyland and

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:50.250
do the touristy stuff can you just take me
to the campus of of of USC just please take

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:57.610
me to the campus and we just drove onto campus
and I went to their bookstore and I took all

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:02.611
the money that I had brought for this trip
and I bought a copy of every book that I could

00:07:02.611 --> 00:07:07.840
afford from the film program literally a stack
of books this big going into my suitcase bought

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.830
every single book and the books that I couldn't
afford to buy didn't have enough money for

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:16.520
I just wrote down the name of the book and
the author and I went back to and I read all

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:21.980
those books and I said if I can't afford this
education and if I cannot afford to go to

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:27.890
this school i'm going to read all the same
books as the as the the the rich kids that

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:31.480
are going to this school i'm just going to
read all the same books so i'll have that

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:36.880
knowledge I took I took that list of books
and those books that I had purchased and I

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:40.500
typed up a list and I passed it out to my
film class and I said hey check it out we

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:44.370
were reading some of those books but not all
of them I said this is what the students at

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:49.490
USC are reading I feel like like i'm just
going to read all these books and that's what

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:57.620
I did oddly enough I dropped out of school
it was really a conflict between I remember

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:03.790
at the time my parents debating over who should
pay for my education I was getting some financial

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:09.440
aid but I was working three jobs I still had
paper routes when I was in college so I worked

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:14.950
at a video store restaurants on the weekends
and I had newspaper routes and getting financial

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:23.040
aid and just trying to do my best to get through
college and it was it was a lot so I finally

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:30.650
just said I can't I can't continue doing this
you know and I that's when I decided I would

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:35.909
just do this thing called film threat because
I could it would give me an excuse to talk

00:08:35.909 --> 00:08:41.269
to filmmakers because they'd have to talk
to me because I was doing this zine right

00:08:41.269 --> 00:08:46.920
and I ended up you know months after the first
issues of film threat came out I dropped out

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:53.000
of college years later ironically I wrote
a book called the ultimate film festival survival

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:58.430
guide which is now for a lot of film programs
in their in part of their independent film

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:04.089
studies my book is required reading so I have
done speaking engagements at certain colleges

00:09:04.089 --> 00:09:08.529
so I just find it funny that here I am like
I dropped out of college dropped out of film

00:09:08.529 --> 00:09:14.850
school never completed my formal education
and now I talk to students who have read my

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:24.990
book which is filled with a lot of no bs advice
about how to launch your your career in independent

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:32.610
film when you saw the list of books for USC
at that time when you had visited la did you

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:36.990
almost feel like you were being led in on
a secret in some way wow when I when I when

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:43.670
I looked at the types of books that that were
part of that program it was kind of like getting

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:50.639
a glimpse into like this secret world because
there was a better balance of there were some

00:09:50.639 --> 00:09:58.079
film theory you know and and you know critical
essay types of books that got into a philosophy

00:09:58.079 --> 00:10:06.240
of film and cinema but then there were more
production books cinematography right production

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:13.230
sound like much more of the much more about
the craft of filmmaking so I felt that their

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:20.019
choices of books had a much better balance
but I was pleased to see some of the some

00:10:20.019 --> 00:10:25.970
of the the philosophical books mixed in there
as well I really think it's like what you're

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:32.360
what you're attracted to I love the philosophy
I also think at the end of the day you know

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:36.640
you got you got to know you got to know how
everything functions and works together you

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:40.950
have to know about editing not just the theory
of editing but how to physically do it so

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:46.740
I so I think that and a lot of trade schools
have cropped up where you can pay you can

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:51.070
pay a sum of money and just get an education
and trade but I feel like you're never not

00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:56.120
done with your education I mean myself even
like i'm always learning something i'm always

00:10:56.120 --> 00:11:03.759
listening to you know filmmaker commentary
and podcasts and and and whatnot i'm i'm always

00:11:03.759 --> 00:11:10.899
i'm always learning it's like that that thirst
for knowledge has never it's never left me

00:11:10.899 --> 00:11:17.040
would you recommend to well maybe this is
too personal but but having gone a route that

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:21.579
wasn't as traditional no no you can go too
okay I mean as someone who also dropped out

00:11:21.579 --> 00:11:28.189
of school and it was mostly because of financial
reasons if I were to have children now I don't

00:11:28.189 --> 00:11:32.149
know what I would advise them but I don't
have kids so I don't have to worry about that

00:11:32.149 --> 00:11:41.029
but just as a parent what did you tell yeah
I you know I really think it's it's it comes

00:11:41.029 --> 00:11:48.839
down to a personal choice I feel like there
is a generation that may have not been given

00:11:48.839 --> 00:11:57.619
enough useful education that would apply to
what what the marketplace is looking for that's

00:11:57.619 --> 00:12:03.970
why I think you see the rise of so many independent
creators whether it be on youtube filmmakers

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:10.259
and whatnot there's so much more opportunity
for independent creators but I do think it's

00:12:10.259 --> 00:12:17.999
important to have that base knowledge you
